From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 12:03:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: lose the device when the Metal command queue
 stops

A signal this driver hands Metal is delivered by the queue it was enqueued
on, so a queue that stops running work never delivers it and never says so.
Nothing else notices: the queue reports nothing further, so the retirement
that replaces a failed queue never triggers, the failure count the signal
recovery keys on stays zero, and every waiter blocks -- in this process, in
the renderer's per-ring sync thread, and through the guest protocol in the
guest.  An iPad Vulkan CTS fraction spent 2,700 seconds in that state, paying
thirty seconds of host escape for every test case, with not one line from the
driver.

The one bound that could have caught it, kk_timeline_recover_lost_signal,
called kk_queue_complete_owed_signals, whose body runs only while a reported
failure is outstanding.  That is right for what it repairs -- a signal a
failure destroyed -- and exactly wrong here, because no failure was reported.

Split the two cases where the evidence separates them.  A queue carrying a
reported failure destroyed the signal, and the repair is unchanged.  A queue
that has reported no failure destroyed nothing, so it accepted the signal and
stopped: that is the disposition kk_queue_error.h already gives a queue that
stops reporting, and the device is lost, with a report naming the value, the
bound and how many submissions the queue has not answered for.  The bound for
losing a device is three times the one for completing a value, because it is
final and must not be reached by a queue that is merely busy.

Both bounds run on the one clock the timeline keeps while it stands still
owing a signal, so waiters on different values of a stopped queue's timeline
reach the bound together; the new stall mode of the timeline wait test holds
two such waiters and requires both to see the device lost.

A query wait had the same blind spot. vkGetQueryPoolResults with WAIT
observes a GPU write directly in memory and bounded that wait only once the
queue had failed, so a query behind a queue that stopped without failing
spun forever. It now loses the device at the same stopped-queue bound,
counted from the last submission the queue answered for, while the queue
holds work it has not answered for; a queue holding nothing still waits as
long as it takes, because the query may belong to work not yet submitted.
The bound moves to kk_queue.h for both waits, and kk_queue_get_progress,
which replaces kk_queue_has_failed, reads the queue's failure history and its
answered and unanswered submissions under one lock. The wait test's new
query mode holds a query behind a submission the queue never answers for and
requires the device lost between 30 and 45 s.

A queue that rejects everything, as the queue of an app iOS has denied
the GPU does, leaves a hole in that evidence. Each rejected commit is
reported within milliseconds and the driver recovers, but a timeline
signal enqueued without a command buffer behind it has no report, and the
queue drops it in silence. The retirement has cleared the failure count
and nothing is unanswered, which is how a stopped queue looks, so the
driver lost the device for it thirty seconds later. On an A15 a guest
drawing with glmark2 was held off the GPU 5 s after the app entered the
background, two seconds past the point where iOS denies it: 38 rejected
commits and 24 retirements, then nothing for 30 s, then VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST
to the guest's GL client, which never drew again. The run held at 3 s
recovered.

A timeline waiter whose signal has been overdue for
KK_QUEUE_PROBE_DELAY_NS, one second, on a queue that reports no failure
and holds nothing it has not answered for, therefore has the queue
committed an empty command buffer (kk_queue_probe), at most one a second.
The probe is an ordinary submission with a serial and a report. A queue
that rejects it reports a failure, the failure completes the signals the
queue owes as any other does, and the wait ends about a second after it
began. A queue that runs it reports nothing and loses nothing, and a
queue held up behind work only the host can release answers it when the
host does. The probe never completes a signal itself, so a queue that has
really stopped still loses the device at the bound above. It commits from
the waiter's thread, so the submit thread holds a mutex for the whole of
kk_queue_submit and the probe takes it with a try: a queue that is being
submitted to is not quiet, and its replacement cannot move under it.

On the iPad the same 5 s hold, and one at 8 s, ended without losing the
device (126 and 128 rejected commits, 80 and 87 retirements, 1 and 2
probes), and the guest drew again 0.3 to 0.4 s after the app returned.
The test queue_denied, which reproduces the denial on a Mac, is the last
topic of the series: it uses the shared test harness, which the series
introduces after this topic.

A query wait needs no probe and no change. A query a rejected submission
was to make available stays unavailable, because its availability write
died with the commit, and the wait for it ends at KK_LOST_WORK_TIMEOUT_NS
with VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST for that call, which is what Vulkan offers for a
result the implementation cannot produce. Zink, the client the iOS hold
protects, cannot reach that bound: it never passes VK_QUERY_RESULT_WAIT_BIT
to vkGetQueryPoolResults, its CPU reads do not wait, its blocking waits
are on the batch's timeline semaphore, which the recovery above
completes, and its vkCmdCopyQueryPoolResults with the bit set is a GPU
copy that reads availability without waiting.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
